nabolic-androgenic steroid. Its article lists voice deepening among the virilizing effects in women, and states that such masculinizing changes can be irreversible.[1]","Women using it for physique purposes have often chosen it because it is described as more anabolic than androgenic, yet its documented risks include voice deepening, hirsutism, menstrual changes, clitoral enlargement and liver effects.[1]"],"infobox":[["Drug class","Anabolic-androgenic steroid (oral)"],["Brand names","Oxandrin, Anavar"],["First described","1962 (Searle); US medical use from 1964"],["US approval","Withdrawn 2023"],["Legal status","US Schedule III; Canada Schedule IV; UK Schedule 4"]],"sections":[{"h":"Voice and virilization risk","paras":["The reported masculinizing effects in women include voice deepening, body and facial hair growth, menstrual changes, male-pattern hair loss and clitoral enlargement, and the source notes these can persist after use stops.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"History and society","paras":["Searle researchers described it in 1962 and it entered US medical use in 1964. Searle discontinued Anavar in 1989 amid publicity over illicit use, and Savient launched Oxandrin in 1995. The FDA withdrew approval in 2023 citing safety and lack of proven efficacy.[1]","Bodybuilders and athletes have used it non-medically, and women are described as choosing it for its milder androgenic profile.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Other risks","paras":["Liver risks include peliosis hepatis and tumors, and it alters blood lipids; it is contraindicated in pregnancy and strongly potentiates warfarin.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found specifically on its effects on voice."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[{"year":"1962","event":"Described by Searle chemists.[1]"},{"year":"1964","event":"Enters US medical use.[1]"},{"year":"1989","event":"Searle discontinues Anavar.[1]"},{"year":"2023","event":"FDA withdraws approval.[1]"}],"refs":[{"title":"Oxandrolone","url":"ht